## Why This Is Asked
Tests a non-trivial partition DP with a non-linear subarray cost and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation or on-the-fly)
- Path reconstruction via predecessor table

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // precompute max for all ranges
  const mx = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){ mx[i][i]=A[i]; for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++) mx[i][j]=Math.max(mx[i][j-1], A[j]); }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = mx[0][i-1]*(i);
  for(let j=2;j<=k;j++){
    for(let i=j;i<=n;i++){
      for(let t=j-1;t<=i-1;t++){
        const cost = dp[t][j-1] + mx[t][i-1]*(i-t);
        if(cost < dp[i][j]){ dp[i][j]=cost; prv[i][j]=t; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], cutPoints: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you optimize space to O(n) per k?
- How to handle equal costs and tie-breaking?

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a non-linear block value that combines sum and minimum, forcing a DP that accounts for subarray metrics and partition points. It also tests reconstruction of exact k segments and handling of large intermediate data.

## Key Concepts

- Non-linear block cost: (sum) * (min)
- Precomputation of all subarray metrics V[l][r]
- DP over prefixes with exactly k blocks
- Path reconstruction from predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space (reducible to O(nk))

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const V = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = Infinity;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      const sum = pref[r+1]-pref[l];
      V[l][r] = sum * mn;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = NEG;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      pre[i][t] = pre[i-1][t];
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + V[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; pre[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = pre[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) with a streaming recomputation of V? 
- How would the approach adapt if k is large and n up to 2e5 (any pruning or heuristic)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests building a DP over subsequences with constraints, requiring a clear state definition and transition over previous indices, plus reconstruction capabilities.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with constraints
- State dp[i] and parent tracking
- Time/space analysis and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSubseqWithDiff(a, d) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(0);
  const par = Array(n).fill(-1);
  let best = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i] = a[i];
    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
      if (Math.abs(a[i] - a[j]) <= d && dp[j] + a[i] > dp[i]) {
        dp[i] = dp[j] + a[i];
        par[i] = j;
      }
    }
    if (dp[i] > best) {
      best = dp[i];
      bestIdx = i;
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct subsequence
  const seq = [];
  let curr = bestIdx;
  while (curr !== -1) {
    seq.push(curr);
    curr = par[curr];
  }
  return { maxSum: best, subsequence: seq.reverse() };
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
Time: O(n²) due to nested loops checking all previous indices
Space: O(n) for dp and parent arrays

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests a practical DP pattern: partitioning a string into k+1 substrings with a sum objective and exact k cuts, including reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact cuts
- Substring value computation via prefix powers of 10
- Backtracking to recover split points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const a = s.split('').map(Number);
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  const pow10 = new Array(n+1).fill(1);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){pref[i] = pref[i-1]*10 + a[i-1]; pow10[i] = pow10[i-1]*10;}
  const val = (l,r)=> pref[r]-pref[l-1]*pow10[r-l+1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][0] = val(1,i); }
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1]===-Infinity) continue;
        const cur = dp[j][t-1] + val(j+1,i);
        if(cur>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=cur; pre[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct using n and k
  let cuts=[]; let i=n, t=k; while(t>0){ const j=pre[i][t]; cuts.push(j); i=j; t--; }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize for very large n (e.g., n up to 1e5)?
- How to handle large sums (big integers) or modulo arithmetic?
- Could you adapt for non-contiguous partitions with penalties?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // end a block at i
      for (let j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (pref[i+1] - pref[j]) * t;
        if (val > dp[i+1][t]) {
          dp[i+1][t] = val;
          par[i+1][t] = j;
        }
      }
      // skip i is implicit via dp[i+1][t] initialization from dp[i][t]
      if (dp[i][t] > dp[i+1][t]) {
        dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t];
        par[i+1][t] = -2; // indicate skip
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to near O(nk) for special A (e.g., non-decreasing) or under additional constraints?
- How would you extend to allow at most k blocks or to recover the exact endpoints efficiently?

